course: dictionary
lesson: 12674
title: Distinctly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Distinctly» is an adverb. It tells you how something is done, or how strongly something is true. It has three main meanings. It can mean clearly and easily heard, seen or remembered: «Speak distinctly so everyone at the back can hear you.»
explain: It can also mean very definitely, without any doubt: «I distinctly remember locking the door.» The speaker is sure. It can mean noticeably or quite: «It was distinctly cold» means it was clearly cold, more than just a bit chilly.
explain: To choose the right meaning, read the words around it. If the sentence is about speaking, hearing or seeing, think "clearly". If someone is sure about something, think "without doubt". If the word is describing a feeling or a temperature, think "noticeably" or "quite".
fact: «Distinctly» means clearly, without doubt, or noticeably.
fact: «Speak distinctly» means speak so that every word can be heard clearly.
fact: «I distinctly remember» means you are sure that you remember something.
fact: «Distinctly unhappy» means clearly and noticeably unhappy: the word makes the feeling stronger.
exercise: Your teacher asks you to «speak distinctly». What does distinctly mean here? | Very quietly, under your breath | Angrily | Quickly, as fast as you can | *Clearly, so every word can be heard
exercise: «I distinctly remember locking the door.» What does distinctly show about the speaker? | *They are completely sure, with no doubt | They are guessing and not sure | They have forgotten all about it
exercise: «It was distinctly cold.» What does distinctly mean here? | Warm and sunny | Cold for only a moment | *Noticeably, quite cold | A little bit warm
test: «She was distinctly unhappy after the match.» What does distinctly mean here? | Happy in a quiet way | *Clearly and noticeably unhappy | Tired and hungry | Not unhappy at all
test: Ella says, «The bell rang distinctly across the playground.» What does distinctly mean? | Far away and faint | *Clearly heard | Very late | Like a shadow
exam: Which meaning fits «I distinctly saw the owl at dusk»? | I saw it by accident and I am not sure | *Without doubt, I am sure I saw it | I saw it in the morning | I did not see the owl at all
